### 💡 4. Best Practices

1.  **Implement a Page Object Model (POM):** As your application grows, maintaining selectors directly within tests becomes cumbersome. Refactor your Playwright tests using the Page Object Model pattern. This involves creating classes (page objects) for each logical part of your application (e.g., `NotesPage.js`) that encapsulate selectors and interactions. This improves test readability, reduces code duplication, and makes tests more resilient to UI changes.

    *   **Example:**
        ```javascript
        // notesPage.js
        export class NotesPage {
            constructor(page) {
                this.page = page;
                this.noteInput = page.locator('#note-input');
                this.addNoteButton = page.locator('#add-note-btn');
                this.notesContainer = page.locator('#notes-container');
                this.noteItem = (text) => page.locator('#notes-container div.note-item', { hasText: text });
            }

            async goto() {
                await this.page.goto('http://localhost:3000');
                await this.page.evaluate(() => localStorage.clear());
                await this.page.reload();
            }

            async addNote(text) {
                await this.noteInput.fill(text);
                await this.addNoteButton.click();
            }

            async getNoteCount() {
                return this.notesContainer.locator('div.note-item').count();
            }

            // ... other actions
        }
        // In your test file:
        // import { NotesPage } from './notesPage';
        // test('should allow adding a new note', async ({ page }) => {
        //     const notesPage = new NotesPage(page);
        //     await notesPage.goto();
        //     await notesPage.addNote('My test note');
        //     await expect(notesPage.noteItem('My test note')).toBeVisible();
        // });
        ```

2.  **Integrate with CI/CD Pipeline:** Automate the execution of your Playwright tests by integrating them into a Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) pipeline (e.g., GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins). This ensures that tests are run automatically on every code push or pull request, catching regressions early in the development cycle and preventing faulty code from reaching production. Ensure your CI/CD setup includes steps to install dependencies, run the application, and then execute the Playwright tests.

3.  **Introduce Unit and Component Testing for `script.js`:** While E2E tests are crucial for verifying the full user flow, they can be slow and brittle. For the logic within `script.js`, consider adding dedicated unit tests (e.g., using Jest or Vitest) to test individual functions (like `addNote`, `deleteNote`, `saveNotesToLocalStorage`, `loadNotesFromLocalStorage`) in isolation. This provides faster feedback on code changes, makes debugging easier, and ensures the core business logic is robust, independent of the UI. This would significantly complement your E2E suite, focusing E2E tests on integration and user experience rather than granular logic.

