### 💡 4. Best Practices

1.  **Introduce `data-testid` Attributes**: The current `index.html` relies heavily on semantic HTML elements, `id`s, and class names for styling, but not necessarily for robust testing. While Playwright's locator strategies are powerful, `data-testid` attributes provide the most stable and resilient selectors for E2E tests.
    *   **Example Improvement**:
        ```html
        <header data-testid="main-header">
            <h1 data-testid="header-title">Pediredla Shyam</h1>
            <p data-testid="header-description">Computer Science Student | Web Developer | Problem Solver</p>
        </header>
        <nav data-testid="main-navigation">
            <a href="#about" data-testid="nav-link-about">About Me</a>
            <!-- ... other links ... -->
        </nav>
        <section id="about" data-testid="section-about">
            <h2 data-testid="about-heading">About Me</h2>
            <p data-testid="about-paragraph">...</p>
        </section>
        <div class="project" data-testid="project-comicol">
            <h3 data-testid="project-title-comicol">Comicol</h3>
            <p data-testid="project-description-comicol">...</p>
            <a href="https://github.com/SpringMan-Sonic/comicol" target="_blank" data-testid="project-link-comicol">View Project</a>
        </div>
        ```
    *   **Benefit**: Isolates test selectors from CSS or JavaScript changes, making tests less brittle and easier to maintain. Playwright explicitly supports `data-testid` via `page.getByTestId()`.

2.  **Implement CI/CD Integration with Playwright Reporting**: Integrate Playwright tests into a Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipeline (e.g., GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins).
    *   **Architecture Improvement**: Set up a workflow that automatically runs these E2E tests on every pull request or push to the main branch.
    *   **Benefit**: Catches regressions early, ensures that new code changes don't break existing functionality, and provides immediate feedback on the health of the application. Configure Playwright to generate an HTML report (`reporter: 'html'`) and publish it as a build artifact for easy review of test results, including screenshots and video recordings of failures.

3.  **Use Environment Variables for Configuration**: The base URL `http://localhost:3030` is hardcoded. While acceptable for a simple project, in a more complex setup, this URL (and other configurations like API keys, different environments) should be managed via environment variables.
    *   **Architecture Improvement**: Utilize a `.env` file or CI/CD secrets to store configuration variables. Playwright can then access these variables (e.g., `process.env.BASE_URL`).
    *   **Benefit**: Enhances flexibility for running tests across different environments (local, staging, production) without modifying the test code. Improves security by keeping sensitive information out of the codebase.

